1
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:05,020
Hello English learners, welcome back to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:05,760
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,020 --> 00:00:06,700
And I'm Erica.

4
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:12,140
And today we're going to go back to the movies, but this time we're going to do it a little bit different.

5
00:00:12,660 --> 00:00:13,060
That's right.

6
00:00:13,480 --> 00:00:19,000
We're going to be listening as two movie critics offer their opinion on the latest release.

7
00:00:19,480 --> 00:00:23,960
Alright, so we're going to be learning how to criticize or give your opinions about something.

8
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:24,860
That's right.

9
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:36,320
And here we're talking movies, but a lot of this language can be used to offer your criticism or ideas on literature or art or other artistic pursuits.

10
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:41,980
Okay, so before we listen to this dialogue for the first time, let's take a look at vocabulary preview.

11
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:45,080
Vocabulary preview.

12
00:00:45,350 --> 00:00:50,610
Well, we're going to look at one word here that we will hear in the dialogue and it is erudite.

13
00:00:51,190 --> 00:00:51,680
Erudite.

14
00:00:52,110 --> 00:00:52,510
Erudite.

15
00:00:52,930 --> 00:00:54,310
Okay, so this word erudite.

16
00:00:54,630 --> 00:00:56,190
Some people pronounce it erudite.

17
00:00:56,510 --> 00:00:56,830
That's true.

18
00:00:57,720 --> 00:01:02,910
And basically it's an adjective describing someone who has a lot of knowledge about a subject.

19
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:06,010
Okay, so an erudite in engineering.

20
00:01:06,650 --> 00:01:07,990
Yes, or an erudite professor.

21
00:01:08,530 --> 00:01:09,310
Okay, very good.

22
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:11,820
So that's the word that we're going to preview today.

23
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:25,520
And now let's listen to our dialogue and find out what movie they are criticizing.

24
00:01:25,520 --> 00:01:28,410
Welcome back movie lovers to another premiere movie review.

25
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:33,690
My name is Richard Clark and I am joined today by the very erudite David Watson.

26
00:01:34,360 --> 00:01:34,810
Thank you, Dick.

27
00:01:35,620 --> 00:01:37,610
Today we're going to talk about the movie Lion King.

28
00:01:38,240 --> 00:01:39,750
Tell me, what is your impression of this film?

29
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:45,030
Well, I think this film is simply a fable depicting man's eternal greed for power.

30
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:47,450
And in my opinion, it's a very fine film.

31
00:01:48,180 --> 00:01:54,070
Even despite the accusations of plagiarism of traditional folk tales from other countries, the musical score was amazing.

32
00:01:54,550 --> 00:01:57,970
The animation was very well done and the story was simply enchanting.

33
00:01:58,120 --> 00:01:58,670
I think otherwise.

34
00:01:59,480 --> 00:02:04,230
Even though the animation was technically strong and as you say, the score and songs performed by Elton John were great.

35
00:02:04,840 --> 00:02:06,710
The film lacks certain originality.

36
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:07,670
It lacked heart.

37
00:02:08,260 --> 00:02:10,190
And I would dare to say it was too predictable.

38
00:02:10,990 --> 00:02:11,350
Predictable?

39
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:12,760
How?

40
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:13,600
Come on, Dave.

41
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:15,120
It's a G-rated movie.

42
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:16,220
It's for the kids.

43
00:02:16,780 --> 00:02:17,680
It's not a thriller.

44
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:18,860
Well, that's just it.

45
00:02:19,480 --> 00:02:21,620
It did have some very dramatic and intense scenes.

46
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:25,700
For example, when Mufasa dies or the dark, grim portrayal of Scar.

47
00:02:26,260 --> 00:02:28,140
Even so, the film is linear.

48
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:29,600
Mufasa dies.

49
00:02:30,180 --> 00:02:31,400
Simba runs away thinking it's his fault.

50
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:34,400
Falls in love and returns to retake what's rightfully his.

51
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:36,280
It's just too cliche.

52
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:37,180
How can it be cliche?

53
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:38,120
It's a fable.

54
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:40,440
It's telling a time-honored story.

55
00:02:41,040 --> 00:02:47,780
The movie makes a point of how the hunger of power leads to corruption and teaches children of the value of respect, life, and love.

56
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:50,660
You have always been so soft, Dick.

57
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:54,300
Open your heart, Dave.

58
00:02:54,960 --> 00:02:55,500
Don't shut us out.

59
00:02:55,960 --> 00:02:57,360
Anyway, that's all for today, folks.

60
00:02:57,960 --> 00:03:00,400
Join us next time as we talk about How to Lose a Guy in 10 Days.

61
00:03:00,760 --> 00:03:02,060
I'm sure you'll love that one, Dick.

62
00:03:09,560 --> 00:03:10,560
All right.

63
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:11,730
So The Lion King, one of my all-time favorites.

64
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:14,850
A very good movie and, well, mixed feelings about it, right?

65
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:17,360
Some people think it's good.

66
00:03:17,360 --> 00:03:19,640
Other people think it's not that good.

67
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:21,330
I'm in the good camp, as you know.

68
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:26,800
But anyway, there's tons of really useful and quite difficult language here.

69
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:29,190
So why don't we look at that now in Language Takeaway.

70
00:03:31,460 --> 00:03:32,620
Language Takeaway.

71
00:03:33,040 --> 00:03:34,040
All right.

72
00:03:34,770 --> 00:03:39,310
On Language Takeaway today, we have five words and we can start with the first one.

73
00:03:39,850 --> 00:03:45,150
So the first critic said that this was a movie depicting man's eternal greed for power.

74
00:03:46,070 --> 00:03:46,550
Depicting.

75
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:47,290
To depict.

76
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:48,800
All right.

77
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:51,750
So this word, depict, it sounds a little bit strange.

78
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:55,570
So before we explain it, let's take a look at some examples.

79
00:03:57,680 --> 00:04:00,200
Example one.

80
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:03,770
I don't really like the way that novel depicted the role of women in society.

81
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:08,440
It was pretty sexist.

82
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:08,700
Example two.

83
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:17,300
A film depicts the realities of war very accurately.

84
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:20,160
Example three.

85
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:24,880
Apparently this image here is depicting some of the most well-known scenes from Australian history.

86
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:25,880
All right.

87
00:04:27,260 --> 00:04:27,640
So depicting.

88
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:31,760
I can basically understand that this means to show something.

89
00:04:32,420 --> 00:04:39,900
Now we can notice that the word depict has this short little word picked in there that sounds like picture.

90
00:04:40,900 --> 00:04:47,060
And when we think about depicting, we're often thinking about in a picture or in a book or in a painting.

91
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:50,800
So in this case, the story is depicted in a movie, right?

92
00:04:51,080 --> 00:04:51,360
That's right.

93
00:04:51,680 --> 00:04:52,400
So you can see it.

94
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:53,440
It's a picture, basically.

95
00:04:53,880 --> 00:04:54,000
Exactly.

96
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:56,240
All right.

97
00:04:56,240 --> 00:04:57,240
Very good.

98
00:04:57,240 --> 00:04:58,240
So to depict.

99
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:03,120
Well, moving on, the critic also talked about the grim portrayal of the villain of the movie, Scar.

100
00:05:03,120 --> 00:05:04,120
That's right.

101
00:05:04,120 --> 00:05:04,960
So grim portrayal.

102
00:05:05,820 --> 00:05:07,100
A grim portrayal.

103
00:05:07,160 --> 00:05:08,200
Okay.

104
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:10,240
Let's look at the word grim first.

105
00:05:10,600 --> 00:05:15,830
When something is grim, it's really unpleasant or uncomfortable or even shocking to think about.

106
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:16,510
Okay.

107
00:05:17,560 --> 00:05:18,560
So we're going to use something that is grim.

108
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:19,250
Now what about portrayal?

109
00:05:19,910 --> 00:05:21,330
Really close to depict.

110
00:05:21,330 --> 00:05:28,950
A portrayal is something that shows or describes something, especially like in a book or in a picture or in a play.

111
00:05:29,430 --> 00:05:29,770
Okay.

112
00:05:29,770 --> 00:05:30,430
So a portrayal.

113
00:05:31,030 --> 00:05:33,330
I guess we can relate it again to a portrait.

114
00:05:33,810 --> 00:05:34,090
That's right.

115
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:36,000
All right.

116
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:37,000
So portrayal.

117
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:38,890
Now we can also use this as a verb, to portray.

118
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:42,370
So this movie portrays man's eternal greed.

119
00:05:42,930 --> 00:05:43,310
Okay.

120
00:05:43,450 --> 00:05:46,490
So we can use this as a noun or as a verb.

121
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:47,240
That's right.

122
00:05:47,540 --> 00:05:51,560
So moving on with our third word today, we have an enchanting movie.

123
00:05:51,940 --> 00:05:52,300
That's right.

124
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:54,500
The story was simply enchanting.

125
00:05:54,660 --> 00:05:54,880
Okay.

126
00:05:55,120 --> 00:05:56,920
So if something is enchanting.

127
00:05:57,460 --> 00:06:03,960
It's really attractive and beautiful and interesting and it holds your attention.

128
00:06:04,360 --> 00:06:04,600
Okay.

129
00:06:04,740 --> 00:06:05,580
It holds your attention.

130
00:06:06,060 --> 00:06:07,240
So it's something very nice.

131
00:06:07,580 --> 00:06:09,360
Something that is enchanting has to be nice.

132
00:06:09,640 --> 00:06:09,840
That's right.

133
00:06:09,980 --> 00:06:12,260
And it has this sense of magic around it.

134
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:12,600
Okay.

135
00:06:13,160 --> 00:06:13,640
Very good.

136
00:06:14,760 --> 00:06:16,120
So an enchanting moment.

137
00:06:16,120 --> 00:06:17,120
Or an enchanting woman.

138
00:06:17,840 --> 00:06:18,840
Okay.

139
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:20,300
And now what about our next word?

140
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:21,640
It's a very...

141
00:06:22,080 --> 00:06:22,880
And what about our next word?

142
00:06:23,160 --> 00:06:25,500
It's French actually, but we use it in English.

143
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:26,380
That's right.

144
00:06:27,240 --> 00:06:29,960
So the critics said that this story was cliché.

145
00:06:29,960 --> 00:06:30,960
Cliché.

146
00:06:30,960 --> 00:06:31,960
Cliché.

147
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:32,380
Okay.

148
00:06:32,380 --> 00:06:34,440
So we hear this all the time.

149
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:35,840
What is a cliché?

150
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:45,500
Well, basically, a cliché is a phrase or an idea that is used so often that it loses its meaning or importance or interest.

151
00:06:46,120 --> 00:06:46,400
Okay.

152
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:52,640
So can you give us some examples of a cliché moment or maybe a phrase that's cliché?

153
00:06:52,830 --> 00:06:52,990
Okay.

154
00:06:53,640 --> 00:06:53,910
Well, how about this one?

155
00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:57,770
When you're breaking up with your boyfriend and you say, honey, it's not you.

156
00:06:58,520 --> 00:06:58,750
It's me.

157
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:00,520
Okay.

158
00:07:00,520 --> 00:07:01,520
That's cliché.

159
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:02,520
It's typical.

160
00:07:02,520 --> 00:07:03,520
Yeah.

161
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:11,440
Or maybe a cliché portrayal of a French man would be a guy riding a bike with a baguette, smoking a cigarette and with a beret on his head.

162
00:07:11,440 --> 00:07:12,440
Okay.

163
00:07:12,440 --> 00:07:13,440
So it's a cliché portrayal.

164
00:07:13,440 --> 00:07:14,440
Very good.

165
00:07:14,440 --> 00:07:15,440
So it's common.

166
00:07:15,440 --> 00:07:16,350
It's even to a certain point a little bit stereotypical.

167
00:07:17,320 --> 00:07:18,320
That's right.

168
00:07:18,320 --> 00:07:19,320
All right.

169
00:07:19,320 --> 00:07:19,980
And what about our last word?

170
00:07:20,460 --> 00:07:22,900
Well, they describe this story as a fable.

171
00:07:23,560 --> 00:07:24,240
Fable.

172
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:25,180
A fable.

173
00:07:25,620 --> 00:07:26,060
Okay.

174
00:07:26,100 --> 00:07:29,380
So when we were small, we all read fables.

175
00:07:29,800 --> 00:07:30,060
That's right.

176
00:07:30,960 --> 00:07:35,440
They are stories about animals that are used to teach people a lesson.

177
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:35,570
Okay.

178
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:37,650
So a fable teaches you a lesson.

179
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:41,960
I think the most famous one is the one of the tortoise and the hare, right?

180
00:07:41,960 --> 00:07:42,960
Yeah.

181
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:44,110
And another good one would be the three little pigs.

182
00:07:44,520 --> 00:07:44,710
Okay.

183
00:07:45,520 --> 00:07:45,770
That's also a fable.

184
00:07:46,520 --> 00:07:47,520
Yep.

185
00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:48,520
Okay.

186
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:56,200
So we've looked at these five great words and now it's time for us to move on to putting it together.

187
00:07:56,200 --> 00:07:56,630
Putting it together.

188
00:07:57,200 --> 00:07:58,200
Okay.

189
00:07:58,980 --> 00:08:08,660
Here we're going to look at some phrases that you can use in a bunch of different ways to help you criticize or give your opinion about a piece of art, like a movie or a book or whatever.

190
00:08:08,980 --> 00:08:09,260
Okay.

191
00:08:10,560 --> 00:08:12,000
Let's start with our first word.

192
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:15,680
So the film critic asked, what is your impression of this film?

193
00:08:16,320 --> 00:08:18,260
What's your impression of?

194
00:08:18,760 --> 00:08:20,760
What's your impression of something, right?

195
00:08:20,960 --> 00:08:21,240
That's right.

196
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:23,120
All right.

197
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:28,720
So before we get into explaining impression, let's listen to some more examples.

198
00:08:28,720 --> 00:08:28,980
Example one.

199
00:08:31,160 --> 00:08:35,040
I don't know where, but I have the impression that we've met before.

200
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:37,950
Example two.

201
00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:43,240
I get the impression that he's a very organized person.

202
00:08:45,720 --> 00:08:46,070
Example three.

203
00:08:48,880 --> 00:08:50,620
Somehow Jim got the impression that I was gay.

204
00:08:51,080 --> 00:08:51,580
Did you tell him that?

205
00:08:53,530 --> 00:08:53,930
Okay.

206
00:08:53,930 --> 00:08:59,670
So I can see that this means basically, what's your opinion or how do you feel about something?

207
00:09:00,030 --> 00:09:00,330
Okay.

208
00:09:00,330 --> 00:09:03,950
So you can have an impression of something or of someone.

209
00:09:04,250 --> 00:09:04,370
Yep.

210
00:09:05,440 --> 00:09:06,440
All right.

211
00:09:06,440 --> 00:09:07,000
And what about the next word?

212
00:09:07,440 --> 00:09:15,440
Well, the critic Richard Clark said that it was a really enjoyable film even despite the accusations of plagiarism.

213
00:09:15,640 --> 00:09:16,020
Okay.

214
00:09:16,020 --> 00:09:18,200
So now we have an interesting phrase here.

215
00:09:18,400 --> 00:09:20,980
The accusations of plagiarism.

216
00:09:21,160 --> 00:09:22,660
Accusations of plagiarism.

217
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:23,920
Okay.

218
00:09:23,920 --> 00:09:25,080
So accusations is a noun, right?

219
00:09:25,080 --> 00:09:26,080
Right.

220
00:09:26,080 --> 00:09:26,720
It's the action of accusing.

221
00:09:27,300 --> 00:09:27,540
Okay.

222
00:09:27,620 --> 00:09:28,560
Of blaming somebody.

223
00:09:29,080 --> 00:09:30,080
Yeah.

224
00:09:30,080 --> 00:09:31,080
All right.

225
00:09:31,080 --> 00:09:32,080
And what about plagiarism?

226
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:32,600
This is an academic term.

227
00:09:33,080 --> 00:09:37,880
If you plagiarize, you copy someone's work and take credit for it.

228
00:09:38,020 --> 00:09:38,260
Okay.

229
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:42,520
So if I copy somebody's book and I say I wrote it, then that's plagiarism.

230
00:09:42,520 --> 00:09:43,520
Yeah.

231
00:09:43,520 --> 00:09:44,520
That's pretty bad.

232
00:09:44,520 --> 00:09:45,920
So accusations of plagiarism.

233
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:49,720
Now we can use this phrase, accusations of something in a bunch of different ways.

234
00:09:49,720 --> 00:09:51,240
So Marco, let's have an example.

235
00:09:51,240 --> 00:09:52,240
All right.

236
00:09:52,240 --> 00:09:57,340
So for example, we can say he was cleared of all accusations of tax evasion.

237
00:09:58,080 --> 00:09:58,360
Okay.

238
00:09:59,280 --> 00:10:02,000
He denied accusations of cheating on his wife.

239
00:10:02,480 --> 00:10:02,800
All right.

240
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:03,280
Very good.

241
00:10:04,320 --> 00:10:05,560
So accusations.

242
00:10:05,560 --> 00:10:11,320
And now moving on to the final part of putting it together, we have this word lacks.

243
00:10:11,320 --> 00:10:13,640
The film lacks a certain originality.

244
00:10:13,810 --> 00:10:14,130
Okay.

245
00:10:14,210 --> 00:10:17,450
So the film lacks a certain originality.

246
00:10:17,590 --> 00:10:18,670
What does he mean by that?

247
00:10:18,950 --> 00:10:20,330
It doesn't have enough of.

248
00:10:20,810 --> 00:10:21,190
Okay.

249
00:10:21,270 --> 00:10:24,470
So if you are lacking something, you don't have enough of it.

250
00:10:24,630 --> 00:10:25,450
Or you don't have it at all.

251
00:10:25,920 --> 00:10:26,920
All right.

252
00:10:26,920 --> 00:10:28,730
So can you give us some more examples of how we can use lack?

253
00:10:29,420 --> 00:10:29,860
Okay.

254
00:10:30,360 --> 00:10:35,400
So the same way it's used in the dialogue, our team lacks the appropriate talent to complete this project.

255
00:10:35,440 --> 00:10:35,920
Okay.

256
00:10:35,920 --> 00:10:40,700
Or I can say, our school lacks infrastructure for so many students.

257
00:10:41,080 --> 00:10:44,440
Or we can also say, infrastructure is lacking at our school.

258
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:45,120
Okay.

259
00:10:45,160 --> 00:10:47,480
So you can use it in those two ways, right?

260
00:10:47,640 --> 00:10:50,120
You can use it as a gerund or as a verb.

261
00:10:50,120 --> 00:10:51,120
That's right.

262
00:10:51,120 --> 00:10:52,120
Okay.

263
00:10:52,120 --> 00:10:52,320
Lots of great language here.

264
00:10:53,120 --> 00:11:04,160
Let's hear it one more time in context with the dialogue.

265
00:11:04,160 --> 00:11:07,090
Welcome back movie lovers to another premiere movie review.

266
00:11:07,760 --> 00:11:12,370
My name is Richard Clark and I am joined today by the very erudite David Watson.

267
00:11:13,040 --> 00:11:13,490
Thank you, Dick.

268
00:11:14,280 --> 00:11:16,290
Today we're going to talk about the movie Lion King.

269
00:11:16,880 --> 00:11:18,430
Tell me what is your impression of this film?

270
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,450
Well, I think this film is simply a fable.

271
00:11:22,080 --> 00:11:24,240
Rising man's eternal greed for power.

272
00:11:24,240 --> 00:11:26,130
And in my opinion, it's a very fine film.

273
00:11:26,840 --> 00:11:32,760
Even despite the accusations of plagiarism of traditional folk tales from other countries, the musical score was amazing.

274
00:11:33,220 --> 00:11:36,640
The animation was very well done and the story was simply enchanting.

275
00:11:36,780 --> 00:11:42,920
I think otherwise, even though the animation was technically strong and as you say, the score and songs performed by Elton John were great.

276
00:11:43,520 --> 00:11:45,380
The film lacks certain originality.

277
00:11:45,720 --> 00:11:46,360
It lacked heart.

278
00:11:46,920 --> 00:11:48,820
And I would dare to say it was too predictable.

279
00:11:49,680 --> 00:11:50,020
Predictable?

280
00:11:50,640 --> 00:11:51,640
How?

281
00:11:51,640 --> 00:11:52,280
Come on, Dave.

282
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:53,640
It's a G-rated movie.

283
00:11:54,280 --> 00:11:54,920
It's for the kids.

284
00:11:55,520 --> 00:11:56,360
It's not a thriller.

285
00:11:56,740 --> 00:11:57,560
Well, that's just it.

286
00:11:58,180 --> 00:12:00,300
It did have some very dramatic and intense scenes.

287
00:12:00,300 --> 00:12:04,360
For example, when Mufasa dies or the dark, grim portrayal of Scar.

288
00:12:04,940 --> 00:12:06,840
Even so, the film is linear.

289
00:12:07,540 --> 00:12:12,640
Mufasa dies, Simba runs away thinking it's his fault, falls in love and returns to retake what's rightfully his.

290
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:13,700
It's just too cliche.

291
00:12:14,590 --> 00:12:15,870
How can it be cliche?

292
00:12:16,190 --> 00:12:16,810
It's a fable.

293
00:12:17,630 --> 00:12:19,130
It's telling a time-honored story.

294
00:12:19,710 --> 00:12:26,470
The movie makes a point of how the hunger for power leads to corruption and teaches children the value of respect, life and love.

295
00:12:27,090 --> 00:12:29,350
You have always been so soft, Dick.

296
00:12:31,960 --> 00:12:32,970
Open your heart, Dave.

297
00:12:33,640 --> 00:12:34,190
Don't shut us out.

298
00:12:34,640 --> 00:12:36,050
Anyway, that's all for today, folks.

299
00:12:36,650 --> 00:12:39,070
Join us next time as we talk about How to Lose a Guy in 10 Days.

300
00:12:39,390 --> 00:12:40,750
I'm sure you'll love that one, Dick.

301
00:12:44,490 --> 00:12:45,810
All right, so a good movie.

302
00:12:46,120 --> 00:12:47,290
What do you think about The Lion King?

303
00:12:47,960 --> 00:12:48,750
What do you think about his critique?

304
00:12:49,250 --> 00:12:51,450
Well, I am with Dick on this one.

305
00:12:51,910 --> 00:12:53,670
I think that this is a great film.

306
00:12:53,800 --> 00:12:57,520
It was one of my favorites and I've seen it about five billion times.

307
00:12:57,520 --> 00:12:57,710
Really?

308
00:12:58,520 --> 00:12:59,520
Yeah.

309
00:12:59,520 --> 00:13:00,520
You really liked it?

310
00:13:00,520 --> 00:13:01,520
But I'm not interested in my opinion.

311
00:13:01,520 --> 00:13:03,910
I want to hear criticisms and film criticisms from our users.

312
00:13:04,530 --> 00:13:04,790
Right.

313
00:13:04,830 --> 00:13:17,490
So come to our website at EnglishPod.com where you can leave your questions, your comments, and also any criticisms on this particular movie or maybe the other one that we mentioned, How to Lose a Guy in 10 Days.

314
00:13:17,790 --> 00:13:21,110
Personally, not so fond of that one, but looking forward to hearing from our users.

315
00:13:21,410 --> 00:13:21,630
All right.

316
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:23,410
Or maybe any other movies you might like.

317
00:13:23,920 --> 00:13:26,950
So come to the website and until next time.

318
00:13:27,450 --> 00:13:27,610
Goodbye.

319
00:13:28,240 --> 00:13:34,760
I'll watch Kill Bill at the end.
